r year Davenport classmates not  most likely to succeed but most likely  to come back from the intramural fields  with bloody knees that was an actual  award they gave out I think it hasn't  been given out since I hope I also tried  my hand at sports reporting for the Yale  Daily News now you might be thinking  Samantha power eventual contributor to  The Economist's and the New Yorker turns  to writing at Yale and discovers she's a  natural well thanks to the internet we  can let some of my early reporting speak  for itself here's how I opened my very  first article in September 1988 just  having arrived on campus quote  volleyballs aren't the only things high  up in the air this week for the women's  volleyball team so our expectations and  spirits end quote  ouch  or consider this I really got better  quickly March 1989 quote the a cappella  groups something extra sang the national  anthem before Saturday nights Yale  Cornell woman women's basketball game  and the blue were well aware that it  would take something extra or rather  something extraordinary for them to win  end quote  really early in my sophomore year I was  briefly given a sports column called  power play whose name judging from my  abuse of puns in the other articles was  probably my idea mercifully it seems  only to have run a couple times but  instead of throwing myself into  journalism I mostly held back I wrote  articles the way I wrote my class papers  last minute without investing the time  or energy it takes to get better when  you risk little you can't be  disappointed if you don't excel you are  not failing to thrive you just aren't  trying these were my unconscious alibis  and then at the end of my freshman year  here a pair of lightning bolts hit the  first came in a class during my freshman  spring professor Victor Lustig was  teaching an advanced seminar on modern  Irish literature which I'd managed to  talk my way into I had signed up for the  course because I'd already read most of  